{"verify_memory_ids":["memory_86","memory_91"],"intended_action":"simplify_onboarding","scale":"standard_experiment","uses_memory_ids":["memory_86","memory_31","memory_91","memory_73"],"downstream_value_risk_flagged":true,"preserves_uncertainty":true,"rationale":"The two declining metrics this quarter are activation (-6%) and onboarding completion (-4%), so the highest-leverage single slot is one that acts directly on the funnel step that is degrading. memory_86 (shortened onboarding lifts completion and time-to-value) is the most directly load-bearing inherited claim for that, reinforced by memory_31 on signup simplification. I am verifying memory_86 because the whole decision rests on it, and memory_91 because activation messaging is the nearest competing use of the same slot and targets the same symptom; if memory_86's original record turns out weak or scoped to a segment that no longer resembles today's traffic, memory_91 is the fallback. I deliberately spend no budget on memory_73: it already carries its own contradiction on its face (signups +31%, revenue +18%, but retention -12% over only four weeks), and discounting into a competitor's aggressive entry pricing trades durable retention for a short revenue spike, which is the opposite of the sustainable-qualified-revenue objective. Verifying it could not plausibly flip me toward it. memory_57 and memory_44 are credible but address segments that are not the ones currently bleeding.

Downstream value risk is real and I am flagging it: simplifying onboarding and cutting required fields tends to remove exactly the qualification and firmographic data that routing, scoring, and enterprise sales-assist (memory_44) depend on. A completion-rate win can therefore be a qualified-revenue loss that shows up one or two quarters later, outside the experiment window. So the experiment must instrument downstream lead quality and 60/90-day retained revenue, not just completion and week-one activation.

Uncertainty that survives: these memories are 15-64 days old, consolidated and summarized rather than read fresh, and none of them were measured under a competitor-pressure regime. The current declines may be driven by a mix shift toward price-sensitive accounts rather than by onboarding friction per se, in which case simplification treats a symptom. I am therefore running this as a standard controlled experiment with a holdout and pre-registered downstream guardrails rather than a broad rollout, and I will not treat a completion-rate lift alone as sufficient to ship.","confidence":66}
